  "sourceType": "CLASSIC",
  "sourceNotes": "Upside-down cakes became an American home-baking staple from the 1920s onward with the popularisation of the pineapple upside-down cake. The technique of cooking fruit in caramelised butter in the base of a tin before adding a sponge batter is older, related to the French tarte tatin and other inverted fruit pastries. The plum version uses the same technique and is particularly popular in late summer and early autumn when Victoria plums are in season. The method is standard and documented across British, American, and French baking references.",
